A percent sign in a document with no header is text in both outputs. The
HTML shows it, and so does the PDF. It rose by 50% in a year, and the
rest of that source line prints too.

# Heading up 50% today

A *bold 20% claim*, **strong 30% claim**, and a [link to 40% off](https://example.com/sale).

A footnote.[fn: Up 10% since 2020.] A classic note.[^c]

[^c]: Classic 15% note.

- List item 60% done
- Second item

| Item | Share |
|---|---|
| A | 70% |

> Quoted 80% of the time.

> [!note] Callout 85% title
> Callout body 88% here.

@begin(figure)[Caption share up 90%]{id=fig:pct}
Figure body.
@end(figure)

@begin(theorem)[Note 95% sure]
Theorem body.
@end(theorem)

An escaped \% stays one percent, and so does 100\%.

A bare address https://example.com/a%20b keeps its percent.
